          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01SaQWQGvkJTonHs7fgL2Q3J",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, including hybrid approaches that merge lexical and semantic search.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Single authoritative source detailing hybrid retrieval; mentions production adoption but not yet independently corroborated.\\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets\u2014often via reciprocal rank fusion\u2014to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\nThis approach consistently outperforms either method alone, particularly on queries that mix:\\n- **Rare identifiers** (error codes, product SKUs, unique names)\\n- **Natural language intent** (semantic meaning and context)\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, reflecting its practical robustness across diverse query types.\\n\\n### Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nReciprocal rank fusion is a standard technique for merging ranked result sets from multiple retrieval methods. It assigns each result a score based on its rank in each result set, then combines these scores to produce a final ranking that balances signals from both lexical and semantic search.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval source (eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-04)\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
